{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"Remove regfile bulk reset\n=========================\n\nWhat changed:\n- Removed the synchronous reset clear from `rtl/reg_file.sv` for x1..x31.\n- Kept x0 hardwired to zero, suppressed writes to x0, and preserved the same-cycle write-first read bypass.\n- Left the `reset` port in place for top-level compatibility and lint-suppressed it locally as unused.\n- Updated `test/test_reg_file.py` so it no longer expects reset to clear all registers. It now checks x0 after reset, writes after reset, dual read ports, and write-first bypass.\n\nDeviations:\n- No functional deviations from the hypothesis. The reset input remains present but does not feed the register storage or read/write datapath.\n\nConcerns:\n- x1..x31 are intentionally uninitialized after reset. This is architectural-safe for RV32I as long as software initializes live state before use, matching the benchmark assumption.\n\nLocal checks:\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: pass.\n- `pytest -q cores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py`: pass.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: pass, 2 ELFs tested.\n\nNotes:\n- Plain `bash formal/run_all.sh` exits before running checks in this checkout because the script requires `RTL_DIR` and `CORE_NAME`; rerun with the target env vars passed.\n- Plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` defaulted the C++ harness to `NRET=2` and failed to compile against this `nret: 1` core. The target-matching `NRET=1` invocation passed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
