{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'nl -ba cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv'","aggregated_output":"     1\t// rtl/forward_unit.sv\n     2\t//\n     3\t// Operand forwarding. For each rs in the EX-stage's source list, picks\n     4\t// where the freshest value lives:\n     5\t//   00 (NONE)    : ID/EX register's rs?_val (= regfile read of one cycle ago)\n     6\t//   01 (EX_MEM)  : the in-flight ALU result from the EX/MEM register\n     7\t//   10 (MEM_WB)  : the WB-stage's regfile-write data\n     8\t//\n     9\t// Priority is EX/MEM > MEM/WB > none (younger writer wins, x0 always 0).\n    10\t//\n    11\t// Latency:        combinational.\n    12\t// RVFI fields:    n/a — feeds rs1_rdata / rs2_rdata via EX-stage muxes.\n    13\tmodule forward_unit (\n    14\t  input  logic [4:0] id_ex_rs1,\n    15\t  input  logic [4:0] id_ex_rs2,\n    16\t  input  logic [4:0] ex_mem_rd,\n    17\t  input  logic       ex_mem_w_en,\n    18\t  input  logic [4:0] mem_wb_rd,\n    19\t  input  logic       mem_wb_w_en,\n    20\t  output logic [1:0] fwd_rs1,\n    21\t  output logic [1:0] fwd_rs2\n    22\t);\n    23\t\n    24\t  // Yosys's Verilog frontend rejects SV-style `function … return …`,\n    25\t  // so the per-rs selection is open-coded in two always_comb blocks.\n    26\t  always_comb begin\n    27\t    if      (ex_mem_w_en && ex_mem_rd != 5'b0 && ex_mem_rd == id_ex_rs1) fwd_rs1 = 2'd1;\n    28\t    else if (mem_wb_w_en && mem_wb_rd != 5'b0 && mem_wb_rd == id_ex_rs1) fwd_rs1 = 2'd2;\n    29\t    else                                                                  fwd_rs1 = 2'd0;\n    30\t  end\n    31\t\n    32\t  always_comb begin\n    33\t    if      (ex_mem_w_en && ex_mem_rd != 5'b0 && ex_mem_rd == id_ex_rs2) fwd_rs2 = 2'd1;\n    34\t    else if (mem_wb_w_en && mem_wb_rd != 5'b0 && mem_wb_rd == id_ex_rs2) fwd_rs2 = 2'd2;\n    35\t    else                                                                  fwd_rs2 = 2'd0;\n    36\t  end\n    37\t\n    38\tendmodule\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"One subtlety I’m accounting for: a “future MEM/WB producer” can be a load that later gets killed by a memory misalignment trap, so the precompute must use the same producer-valid notion the old EX-time forwarding effectively saw after MEM. I’m adding that gate in the top-level wiring instead of letting the registered select blindly trust every load-looking writer."}}
