{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"test -f cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && sed -n '1,200p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"Register the IF/ID boundary\n\nChanged:\n- `if_stage.sv` now has a real IF/ID register for fetched PC, instruction, and valid.\n- Redirects still override stalls, update the fetch PC to the redirect target, and clear IF/ID.\n- Stalls hold both the fetch PC and IF/ID register instead of reusing live `io_imemData`.\n- `hazard_unit.sv` was retimed so imem stalls hold IF/ID and inject a decode bubble, load-use still injects one bubble, and dmem/divider stalls keep their whole-pipeline hold priority.\n- Updated stale comments in `core.sv` and `core_pkg.sv`.\n\nDeviations:\n- No new top-level control wires were added. The existing `stall_if`, `flush_if`, `stall_id`, and `flush_id` signals were sufficient after redefining `stall_if` as a registered IF/ID hold.\n- The implementation is conservative on imem stalls: even if IF/ID already contains a valid instruction, decode waits until fetch can refill. This preserves simple no-duplicate sequencing at the cost of extra front-end bubbles.\n\nConcerns:\n- Taken redirects now pay the expected extra registered-boundary bubble.\n- The conservative imem-stall policy may cost IPC under the random stall model. It was chosen to keep the retime low-risk and timing-friendly.\n\nLocal checks:\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv` passed.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh` passed: 53 passed, 0 failed.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1` passed: 2 ELFs tested. Plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` built the shared harness with its default `NRET=2` and failed before simulation because this core declares `nret: 1`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
