{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_65","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"test -f cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"## Forward Ready Loads Into EX\n\nChanged the existing five-stage pipeline to allow an aligned, ready load in\nMEM to feed the immediately following EX-stage instruction without the old\nblanket load-use bubble.\n\nWhat changed:\n- `mem_stage.sv` now exposes `load_fwd_data` and `load_fwd_valid` after\n  byte/halfword extraction, sign/zero extension, misalignment checks, trap\n  control, and `dmem_ready` qualification.\n- `forward_unit.sv` treats EX/MEM loads as forwardable only when that\n  MEM-stage load-forward valid bit is high. Non-load EX/MEM producers keep\n  the existing priority over MEM/WB.\n- `core.sv` muxes the EX/MEM forwarding data between ALU result and ready\n  load data, removes the old raw-fetch load-use wiring, and suppresses\n  branch/JALR redirect side effects while a source-dependent EX/MEM load is\n  still waiting for `io_dmemReady`.\n- `hazard_unit.sv` no longer creates an unconditional load-use interlock;\n  dmem stalls and divider stalls still hold the pipeline.\n- `ex_stage.sv` avoids starting a DIV/REM operation while EX is held by a\n  dmem stall, preventing a held load-dependent divide from capturing stale\n  operands.\n- `test_pipeline.py` adds focused checks for ready load-use forwarding,\n  stalled-load waiting, load-to-store-data forwarding, misaligned load\n  non-forwarding, and a stalled-load branch redirect regression.\n\nDeviations:\n- Added a small top-level redirect gate for the stalled load-dependent\n  branch/JALR case. Without it, the EX/MEM register was correctly held during\n  dmem stalls, but the combinational redirect output could still update the\n  fetch PC using stale operands.\n\nConcerns:\n- The new ready-load bypass puts the selected `io_dmemRData` load extraction\n  path into the EX operand muxes. This is the expected Fmax risk of the\n  hypothesis.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench` in this checkout invokes the harness with its\n  default `NRET=2` and fails to compile the intentionally nret=1 core. The\n  nret-correct run was performed with `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`.\n\nLocal status:\n- Verilator lint: pass.\n- Cocotb pipeline test: pass.\n- Formal: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed, using `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl\n  CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`.\n- Cosim: pass with `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
