{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -n 80 cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r1s0 (improvement, +781.60%): Moving RV32M DIV/REM from the combinational ALU into an iterative EX-stage unit removed the inferred one-cycle divider/remainder network from CoreMark's common forwarding-to-ALU timing path, so rare divide stalls were overwhelmed by the Fmax gain.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r1s1 (regression, +696.04%): After DIV/REM are already off the EX critical path, moving MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU behind the same multi-cycle full-pipeline stall trades away CoreMark's useful single-cycle/DSP multiply throughput for little extra timing relief.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 (regression, +2.64%): An 8-entry direct-mapped BTB with tag/counter lookup in the IF PC-select path did not earn back its fetch-side timing/area cost on CoreMark, so recurring taken-branch bubbles were not the binding bottleneck after the RV32M timing fixes.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r2s0 (improvement, +7.26%): Feeding load-use detection from raw imem rs fields and gating them with fetch-valid/redirect-kill signals keeps IF's flush-to-NOP mux out of the EX redirect-to-ID enable loop, making that hazard path easier to time without changing pipeline behavior.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, -4.76%): A full registered IF/ID boundary added redirect/imem bubble latency without enough extra relief on the regfile/ID-to-EX-redirect hazard/control path after raw-rs load-use gating, so CoreMark favored the lighter combinational fetch/decode boundary.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r2s2 (regression, -16.72%): Stateless backward-branch/JAL predecode still puts imem_data decode, immediate assembly, target adders, and alignment muxing on the IF PC-select path, so its CoreMark bubble savings can be outweighed by fetch-side Fmax loss even without BTB state.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r3s0 (regression, -1.67%): After raw-rs load-use gating, removing IF's flush-to-NOP instruction mux only moved bubble control into `in.valid` gates on ID regfile addresses, ID/EX zeroing, and EX redirect quali\n…[8995 of 12067 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9e277db9fff1132f]…\nrwarding-to-adder/address cone, and the extra op decode plus 32-bit residual select can cost more than Gowin's original case muxing.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r13s1 (broken, n/a): Splitting EX into operand-capture and ALU stages without a MEM-stage load-data bypass extends load-use hazards to one-gap consumers, so the interlock must track load rd through EX/MEM or hold EX1 until MEM/WB rather than only stalling adjacent uses.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r13s2 (regression, -7.19%): Predicting EX/MEM loads as aligned removed low-bit misalign checks from forwarding eligibility but pushed late MEM/WB write-enable validation into the EX operand mux, so CoreMark lost more bypass-cone timing than the shallower rd/rs select saved.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r14s0 (regression, -8.47%): Moving JAL/JALR link selection from EX to WB saved a rare jump-result mux but added a `pc + 4` adder and `is_jump` select to the shared `wb_w_data` forwarding path, so late writeback mux cost outweighed EX result-path cleanup.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
