{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r14s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r14s1\ntitle: \"Load-aware execute retime\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Recent timing still clusters around the ID/EX operand path through EX\n  forwarding, the ALU, and MEM-facing result routing. Most smaller sideband\n  and predictor attempts added control without cutting that cone at a real\n  register boundary, while the prior execute split failed because load-use\n  handling was not extended to the deeper pipe. A corrected execute retime is\n  still the highest-upside structural move because it attacks the dominant\n  path rather than adding another fetch or result-side detour.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split execute into a registered operand-capture stage followed by an\n  arithmetic/address stage, but make load latency explicit in the hazard\n  contract. The first stage resolves the existing forwarding choices, captures\n  rs1/rs2, store data, AUIPC/JALR operands, immediate/control/RVFI metadata,\n  and any DIV/REM start state into an EX1/EX2 bundle. The second stage runs\n  the current ALU, branch comparator, jump target calculation, single-cycle\n  MUL path, DIV/REM result handoff, and EX/MEM register write from those\n  captured operands.\n\n  Correctness is preserved by tracking load-producing rd values through the\n  new EX boundary and EX/MEM. A consumer is held in ID/EX until the load can\n  be forwarded from MEM/WB, or, if an implementation chooses the faster but\n  riskier option, until an exact MEM-stage load-data bypass is available.\n  This directly fixes the r13s1 failure mode where one-gap load consumers\n  advanced too early. Ordinary ALU chains should still forward from the\n  arithmetic stage, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB into operand capture so they do not\n  gain a blanket extra stall. Branches resolve one stage later and some load\n  dependences may pay another bubble, but the expected Fmax win comes from\n  removing forwarding muxes, raw regfile routing, and broad ID/EX fanout from\n  the same timed cone as the 32-bit ALU and dmem address path.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add an EX1/EX2 pipeline bundle carrying captured operands, store data, PC, immediate, decoded control, rd/rs metadata, and load-producer tracking bits.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Restructure execute into operand-capture and arithmetic sub-stages, with ALU, branch, jump, MUL, and DIV/REM result handoff driven from registered operands.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Update forwarding selection so operand capture can choose exact values from the arithmetic stage, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB without adding a blanket ALU-use interlock.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Extend load-use interlock logic across the deeper execute pipe, holding consumers until a load reaches MEM/WB or an exact MEM-stage load bypass is available.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the new execute-stage boundary, added forwarding sources, and corrected load-pending hazard signals while preserving the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add dependency tests covering adjacent and one-gap load consumers, ALU forwarding through the new execute boundary, branch redirects, dmem stalls, and DIV/REM holds.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
