{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"test -f cores/bench/LESSONS.md && sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r1s0 (improvement, +781.60%): Moving RV32M DIV/REM from the combinational ALU into an iterative EX-stage unit removed the inferred one-cycle divider/remainder network from CoreMark's common forwarding-to-ALU timing path, so rare divide stalls were overwhelmed by the Fmax gain.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r1s1 (regression, +696.04%): After DIV/REM are already off the EX critical path, moving MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU behind the same multi-cycle full-pipeline stall trades away CoreMark's useful single-cycle/DSP multiply throughput for little extra timing relief.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 (regression, +2.64%): An 8-entry direct-mapped BTB with tag/counter lookup in the IF PC-select path did not earn back its fetch-side timing/area cost on CoreMark, so recurring taken-branch bubbles were not the binding bottleneck after the RV32M timing fixes.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r2s0 (improvement, +7.26%): Feeding load-use detection from raw imem rs fields and gating them with fetch-valid/redirect-kill signals keeps IF's flush-to-NOP mux out of the EX redirect-to-ID enable loop, making that hazard path easier to time without changing pipeline behavior.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, -4.76%): A full registered IF/ID boundary added redirect/imem bubble latency without enough extra relief on the regfile/ID-to-EX-redirect hazard/control path after raw-rs load-use gating, so CoreMark favored the lighter combinational fetch/decode boundary.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r2s2 (regression, -16.72%): Stateless backward-branch/JAL predecode still puts imem_data decode, immediate assembly, target adders, and alignment muxing on the IF PC-select path, so its CoreMark bubble savings can be outweighed by fetch-side Fmax loss even without BTB state.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r3s0 (regression, -1.67%): After raw-rs load-use gating, removing IF's flush-to-NOP instruction mux only moved bubble control into `in.valid` gates on ID regfile addresses, ID/EX zeroing, and EX redirect qualification, so the added valid fanout did not buy CoreMark timing relief.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (regression, -3.55%): Late-killing redirects only through `fetch_kill_for_hazard` and `flush_id` left redirect's flush fanout in the ID/EX control path while exposing wrong-path `imem_data` to decode/regfile addresses, so removing redirect from IF's instruction mux did not improve CoreMark timing.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r3s2 (regression, -13.86%): Even an EX-trained one-entry backedge predictor keeps a PC tag compare/target mux in IF and adds `pred_next_pc` mismatch plumbing through ID/EX, so CoreMark's loop-bubble savings still lost to fetch/control-path timing cost.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r4s0 (regression, -16.78%): Sharing RV32M high-half multiplies through one unsigned product plus sign-correction subtractors moves 32-bit carry chains after the DSP output on the one-cycle ALU path, so reduced multiplier duplication can lose more Fmax than it saves on CoreMark.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r4s1 (regression, -11.89%): A fall-through two-entry fetch skid buffer moves load-use detection onto the buffer head and adds `fetch_consume`/valid/full control in IF, so CoreMark's chance to overlap backend holds with random imem stalls did not repay the extra fetch/decode control cost.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r4s2 (regression, -28.11%): Retiming a tiny BTB into a registered lookahead still adds IF table update/lookup state plus `pred_pc_next` compare plumbing through ID/EX, so avoiding same-cycle PC-select compares did not make CoreMark's taken-branch bubble savings worth the extra fetch/control cost.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r5s0 (regression, -4.21%): Removing the regfile x1..x31 synchronous clear did not relieve the reported ER-control endpoint, suggesting Gowin's reset/enable packing was not the real CoreMark limiter and the surrounding ID/EX/IF control/routing dominated instead.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
