e-cycle write-first bypass. Leave the reset input present\nor locally lint-suppressed so the top-level wiring need not change, but do\nnot feed reset into every GPR bit. Update the local cocotb regfile test to\nstop requiring non-architectural reset-to-zero behavior and instead verify\nx0, normal writes after reset, dual reads, and write-first bypass.\n\nThis should directly attack the current regfile ER/control endpoint and\nremove a high-fanout reset tree from 31x32 bits without adding any pipeline\nstate or changing CoreMark cycles. If Gowin can pack the regfile as simpler\ndistributed storage or flops with only write enables, the Fmax gain can be\nlarge enough to clear the acceptance threshold while preserving RVFI and ISA\nbehavior.\n\nimplementation_notes: \n\n=== hyp-20261003-001-r7s0 Precompute branch targets in ID ===\noutcome regression\nfitness 118.85\ndelta_pct 3.7\nfmax_mhz 53.384\ncycles 4491817\nlut4 2734\nff 724\nlogic_levels 13\ncritical_path {'from': 'c\n…[4024 of 7096 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:802f73630c018cdd]…\ne mostly lost Fmax. The remaining repeated timing shape\nis still an execute/control cone feeding memory-side outputs or fetch control.\nLoads, stores, and JALR only need rs1+imm, but today they share the same\ngeneral ALU result structure that also contains shifts, compares, and the\nsingle-cycle multiply outputs.\n\nhypothesis: Restructure EX into two parallel lanes without changing pipeline depth: a\nnarrow address/control lane that computes rs1+imm for LOAD/STORE/JALR and\na general ALU lane for arithmetic/logical results. Memory operations should\nwrite the address-lane sum directly into the EX/MEM address result, and JALR\nshould use the same lane for redirect target generation. Keep MUL-class\noperations single-cycle, but keep their products out of the address/control\nlane so common LSU and redirect paths do not traverse the full ALU result\nmux. This should preserve CoreMark cycles while shortening the fwd