{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r12s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-001-r12s0\ntitle: \"Add/sub fast ALU slice\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion's worst path is a short but still timing-limited cone\n  through the ID/EX operand path, EX operand muxing, the monolithic ALU case,\n  and the dmem-facing address/result path. Prior attempts show that adding\n  prediction, extra target adders, memory sideband registers, or wider\n  writeback state tends to lose to routing. The most common CoreMark timed\n  path for loads, stores, ADD, SUB, ADDI, AUIPC, and JALR target formation is\n  still just a 32-bit add/subtract, but it currently shares the same ALU result\n  mux with shifts, compares, logic, and single-cycle multiply.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split the execute datapath into a narrow add/sub fast slice and a residual\n  ALU slice without changing the pipeline or any instruction latency. Decode\n  should carry one compact ID/EX bit for \"uses the add/sub slice result\"\n  covering ADD, SUB, ADDI, AUIPC, load/store address generation, and JALR\n  target formation. EX should compute add/sub directly from the existing\n  forwarded operands and select it with a shallow two-way mux for the common\n  result/address path, while the existing alu.sv handles LUI pass-through,\n  logic, shifts, compares, and single-cycle MUL-class operations only when the\n  compact bit is clear.\n\n  This deliberately avoids a duplicate memory-address adder plus mem-op mux,\n  which already regressed, and instead makes the already-present arithmetic\n  operation the first-class fast result for most CoreMark integer and memory\n  instructions. The expected win is lower LUT depth and cleaner placement on\n  the RF/ID-to-EX-to-dmem cone while preserving CoreMark cycles, the\n  single-cycle DSP multiply path, DIV/REM stalls, RVFI ordering, and all\n  misalignment/trap behavior.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add a compact ID/EX control bit identifying operations whose architectural EX result is the add/sub fast-slice value, without widening unrelated pipeline state.\"\n  - file: rtl/decoder.sv\n    description: \"Set the fast add/sub result bit for ADD/SUB/ADDI, AUIPC, load/store address generation, and JALR target formation while keeping decoder illegal defaults intact.\"\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Leave non-add/sub ALU operations here, or make ADD/SUB benign fallbacks, so the residual ALU no longer dominates the common add/address result select.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compute the forwarded add/sub result beside the residual ALU and select the fast-slice value through a shallow mux for common result/address-producing instructions.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add focused dependency and memory-address tests for ADD/SUB/ADDI, loads/stores, branches around add results, and MUL/DIV coexistence to guard the retimed result select.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
