{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'rg -n \"hyp-20261003-002-r12s1|hyp-20261003-001-r12s0|hyp-20261003-001-r8s0\" cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "23:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261003-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Dedicated load-store address adder\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current champion's timing is dominated by the decode/register-file through EX/front-end region, and several recent worst paths still pass through `cpu/u_ex/u_alu` before reaching dmem-facing logic. Prior attempts show that adding predictor state, skid buffering, or broad ID precompute costs more than it saves, while moving MUL out of the single-cycle path hurts CoreMark throughput. A narrower EX datapath specialization that preserves IPC is the most promising remaining micro-opt.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current champion's timing is dominated by the decode/register-file through EX/front-end region, and several recent worst paths still pass through `cpu/u_ex/u_alu` before reaching dmem-facing logic. Prior attempts show that adding predictor state, skid buffering, or broad ID precompute costs more than it saves, while moving MUL out of the single-cycle path hurts CoreMark throughput. A narrower EX datapath specialization that preserves IPC is the most promising remaining micro-opt.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the existing pipeline, stall behavior, divider, and single-cycle multiply path, but add a dedicated 32-bit effective-address adder in EX for LOAD and STORE instructions. Today memory operations compute `rs1 + imm` through the full ALU module and then carry that result into EX/MEM, so dmem address timing pays for the ALU case muxing and nearby M-extension result cone even though memory ops only need addition. For `ctrl.mem_read || ctrl.mem_write`, select the dedicated address-adder result into `reg_q.alu_result`; all arithmetic, AUIPC/LUI, jumps, branches, and M-extension instructions keep their existing ALU/redirect behavior.\\n\\nCoreMark's timed loop is load/store heavy, and the FPGA score uses randomized dmem stalls, so shortening the address path into the memory stage can improve median Fmax without changing iterations/cycle. The change avoids new IF control, extra ID/EX payl\n…[12097 of 15169 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c7b116788e2b34df]…\n.24, \"vs_baseline\": 914.36, \"fmax_mhz\": 55.221, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 2786, \"ff\": 688, \"seeds\": [52.039, 55.221, 55.221], \"perturbations\": [[0, 52.039, 18], [1, 55.221, 19], [2, 55.221, 19]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_ER_s195\", \"slack_ns\": -14.216, \"arrival_ns\": 24.511, \"logic_cells\": 21, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_if\", \"cpu/u_rf\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 18, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-03T19:19:46.763596Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 12, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r12s1 (regression, +2.24%): Exposing the ALU add sum as a dedicated EX/MEM `mem_addr` sideband leaves the carry-chain operand fanout in EX and adds 32 bits of pipeline/address-state routing, so the dmem path does not improve unless the old ALU-result dependency is actually removed from the critical cone.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
