evels. The ALU implements RV32M\\nDIV/REM using SystemVerilog / and %, forcing Gowin to time a full\\nsingle-cycle divider/remainder network even though CoreMark rarely needs\\nthose operations in the measured loop. Removing that arithmetic from the\\none-cycle EX path should raise Fmax far more than the occasional multi-cycle\\ndivide can hurt iterations per cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases in rtl/alu.sv with a s\n…[201563 of 204635 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ff6c14fd247f7071]…\nrtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the one-entry posted-store predictor, retire predicted-posted RAM stores once, drain them through a registered dmem arbiter, and fence MMIO marker/UART stores.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace full-pipeline dmem stalls for predicted-posted stores with a store-post hold only when the buffer is full, a younger memory op appears, or an MMIO/marker store must complete exactly.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire posted-store full/drain/conflict signals between MEM and hazard control while preserving the existing imem, dmem, and RVFI port contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_posted_store_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add focused tests for stalled RAM stores retiring once, later drain ordering, same-address load/store serialization, and MMIO marker stores never being posted.\"}], \"outcome\": \"placement_failed\", \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": \"placement_failed\", \"seeds\": [null, null, null], \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 14}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"A useful clue: the current champion’s worst path still lands on the memory-side result path with only about 12 logic levels. The near-miss in r12s1 gained a couple percent by splitting memory address state, but the lesson says it left the old ALU-result dependency alive; a stricter version that actually removes that dependency looks like the cleanest micro-opt left."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r12s1\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, (.implementation_notes//\\\"\\\"), (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r12s1\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, (.implementation_notes//\\\"\\\"), (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"The current champion's remaining worst path still terminates at the dmem BRAM interface after passing through ID/EX, the EX operand muxes, and the monolithic ALU result path. Prior predictor and fetch-buffer attempts consistently lost to added routing, while the one recent structural win came from removing bypass work from this same EX cone. The next narrow structural target is to stop load/store addresses from sharing the full ALU writeback-result mux that also serves jumps, shifts, compares, and multiply-class results.\n\nRestructure the EX to MEM handoff so memory operations carry a dedicated registered effective-address field sourced from the ALU's add result before the final operation-result mux. Keep the normal alu_result field for register writeback and branch/jump bookkeeping, but have mem_stage drive io_dmemAddr and RVFI memory address generation from the registered mem_addr field whenever mem_read or mem_write is set.\n\nThis deliberately avoids the earlier failed wide memory sideband retime: do not precompute byte masks, store-data replication, or misalignment traps in EX, and do not add a duplicate address adder. Instead, expose the existing ADD sum as a narrow side output from the ALU/ex_stage and register only that address alongside EX/MEM. The expected benefit is a shorter memory-address cone into the timed dmem interface with unchanged CoreMark cycles, unchanged one-cycle MUL throughput, unchanged load-use behavior, and no fetch/control predictor cost.\n\n\n{\"from\":\"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s\",\"to\":\"cpu/u_rf/regs_ER_s195\",\"slack_ns\":-14.216,\"arrival_ns\":24.511,\"logic_cells\":21,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_if\",\"cpu/u_rf\"]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-001-r13s0\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, (.implementation_notes//\\\"\\\"), (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-001-r13s0\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, (.implementation_notes//\\\"\\\"), (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"The current accepted core is still limited by a short path through ID/EX,\nthe EX operand muxes, rtl/alu.sv, and the MEM-facing result/address cone.\nCoreMark's hottest timed uses of that cone are ordinary ADD-family results\nand load/store effective addresses, but the current ALU presents ADD as one\narm of a broad RV32IM result case that also includes shifts, compares, LUI,\nand single-cycle MUL-class results. Prior attempts to add a separate fast\nadd slice regressed because they widened ID/EX control and duplicated\ndatapath routing; the remaining lower-risk target is the mux shape inside\nthe existing ALU boundary.\n\nRestructure rtl/alu.sv as a hot-path-biased mux tree without changing the\nALU interface, pipeline state, op encodings, or any instruction latency.\nCompute the ADD/SUB carry-chain result once, select ALU_ADD (and optionally\nALU_SUB) through an explicit first-level two-way path, and put the residual\noperations behind a separate non-add case tree. Keep the existing DSP-backed\nMUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU behavior and the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions\nbit-identical, but keep their result muxing off the common ADD/address data\npath as much as the Gowin mapper will honor.\n\nThis should preserve CoreMark cycles while shaving one or more LUT/mux\nlevels from the RF/forwarding-to-ALU-to-dmem path that dominates recent\ntiming reports. Unlike the failed fast-add sideband attempts, it adds no new\nID/EX bits, no duplicate adder, no memory sideband register, and no predictor\nrepair plumbing; the risk is mainly that synthesis flattens the mux back to\nthe old shape, in which case the circuit should remain functionally neutral.\n\n\n{\"from\":\"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\",\"to\":\"dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s\",\"slack_ns\":-13.561,\"arrival_ns\":23.856,\"logic_cells\":12,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\"cpu/u_ex\"]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
