{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r4s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-001-r4s0\ntitle: \"Share RV32M multiply hardware\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion already removed DIV/REM from the one-cycle ALU path, but\n  rtl/alu.sv still builds three independent 32x32 products for MUL, MULH,\n  MULHU, and MULHSU. Prior results show that making all multiply operations\n  multi-cycle loses useful CoreMark throughput, so the better micro-opt target\n  is reducing the multiplier cone without changing single-cycle MUL latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the signed*signed and signed*unsigned multiplier instances with one\n  unsigned 32x32 product and simple high-half sign correction:\n  MUL uses the low unsigned product, MULHU uses the high unsigned product,\n  MULH subtracts b when a is negative and subtracts a when b is negative, and\n  MULHSU subtracts b only when a is negative. Keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\n  substitutions unchanged. This should reduce DSP/LUT routing and the ALU\n  result mux fan-in while preserving CoreMark's single-cycle low multiply path,\n  improving Fmax without increasing measured cycles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Use a single unsigned 32x32 multiplier for all MUL-class operations and derive signed high-half results with RV32M-correct sign correction.\"\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: \"Add or emphasize signed high-half multiply edge vectors to guard the shared-multiplier correction formulas.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
