{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r3s1\\\") | {id,title,motivation,hypothesis:.hypothesis.hypothesis,expected_impact,changes,outcome,fitness,fmax_mhz,critical_path,lesson} | tostring' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"{\"id\":\"hyp-20261003-002-r3s1\",\"title\":\"Late kill redirected fetch\",\"motivation\":\"After DIV/REM were moved out of the ALU and load-use detection was fed from\\nraw imem register fields, the champion's remaining worst path still runs\\nthrough ID/EX branch resolution, IF control, and back into the ID/regfile\\narea. A full registered IF/ID boundary already proved too expensive in IPC,\\nso the promising structural target is the narrower redirect-to-decode\\nfeedback that still exists because IF muxes the ID-facing instruction to a\\nNOP on redirect even though ID/EX is separately flushed.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Change redirect recovery from \\\"inject a NOP instruction in IF and also flush\\nID\\\" to a late-kill structure: IF redirects the PC, but the ID-facing\\ninstruction bits are only forced invalid on imem stalls; redirect kills the\\nwrong-path instruction through the existing ID/EX flush and explicit\\nfetch-kill gating in hazard_unit. This keeps the combinational IF/ID boundary\\nand its current CoreMark IPC behavior, while removing EX redirect from the\\nIF instruction mux and from the regfile read-address cone. The expected win\\nis a cleaner FPGA timing path with no new predictor state, no additional\\nbranch bubbles, and no change to RVFI retirement semantics.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":6,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Decouple redirect from the ID-facing NOP/valid mux: redirect still updates the PC, while only imem-stall style invalid fetches force a NOP payload.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Keep redirect as a fetch-kill and ID/EX flush reason, but stop using it as an IF payload flush reason so wrong-path instructions are killed later by valid/flush control.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Wire the revised IF payload flush control separately from the redirect PC control while preserving the nret=1 top-level IO and RVFI contract.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Rely on the existing ID/EX flush to squash redirected wrong-path payloads, with no new pipeline register or retirement behavior change.\"}],\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":110.54,\"fmax_mhz\":49.654,\"critical_path\":{\"from\":\"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_2_s0\",\"to\":\"dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s\",\"slack_ns\":-15.752,\"arrival_ns\":26.046,\"logic_cells\":16,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_fwd\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\"cpu/u_ex\"]},\"lesson\":\"- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (regression, -3.55%): Late-killing redirects only through `fetch_kill_for_hazard` and `flush_id` left redirect's flush fanout in the ID/EX control path while exposing wrong-path `imem_data` to decode/regfile addresses, so removing redirect from IF's instruction mux did not improve CoreMark timing.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
