{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r2s1\\\") | {id,title,hypothesis:.hypothesis.hypothesis,outcome,fitness,fmax_mhz,critical_path,lesson} | tostring' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"{\"id\":\"hyp-20261003-002-r2s1\",\"title\":\"Register the IF ID boundary\",\"hypothesis\":\"Convert the fetch/decode boundary into a conventional registered IF/ID stage.\\nThe IF stage should hold a fetched instruction, PC, and valid bit in flops,\\nupdate that register only when decode can accept a new instruction, and flush\\nit on redirects. The ID/EX register then consumes the registered IF/ID payload\\ninstead of the live imem_data/redirect mux output, while the hazard unit is\\nretimed so imem stalls hold IF/ID and decode stalls no longer create a\\ncombinational loop through EX redirect and IF flush. Existing single-channel\\nRVFI ordering, load-use bubbles, dmem whole-pipeline holds, divider busy\\nholds, and misaligned/trap behavior remain unchanged. The expected result is a\\nhigher median Fmax by cutting the current ID-EX-IF-hazard-ID feedback path;\\nCoreMark may pay an extra bubble on taken redirects, but the present path is\\ntiming-dominant enough that a substantial Fmax gain should outweigh the IPC\\nloss.\\n\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":101.76,\"fmax_mhz\":50.363,\"critical_path\":{\"from\":\"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0\",\"to\":\"dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s\",\"slack_ns\":-15.05,\"arrival_ns\":25.345,\"logic_cells\":14,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_fwd\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\"cpu/u_ex\"]},\"lesson\":\"- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, -4.76%): A full registered IF/ID boundary added redirect/imem bubble latency without enough extra relief on the regfile/ID-to-EX-redirect hazard/control path after raw-rs load-use gating, so CoreMark favored the lighter combinational fetch/decode boundary.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
