 hyp-20261003-001-r3s0 (regression, -1.67%): After raw-rs load-use gating, removing IF's flush-to-NOP instruction mux only moved bubble control into `in.valid` gates on ID regfile addresses, ID/EX zeroing, and EX redirect quali\n…[4982 of 8054 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:71d305f105073299]…\nannuls on forward-taken and loop-exit mismatches plus `repair_redirect_q` control outweighed the Fmax relief.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r9s0 (regression, -3.37%): Replacing the binary ALU case with one-hot ID/EX controls forced 19 32-bit result masks plus a wide OR combine in EX, so the saved opcode compares did not offset the added select/routing cost on CoreMark's ALU-to-dmem path.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r9s1 (regression, -5.92%): Letting invalid pipeline payloads retain stale values forced extra `valid` gating on load-use, forwarding, redirect, and dmem side-effect paths, so reducing blanket ID/EX/EX/MEM/MEM/WB clears did not relieve CoreMark's dominant control-routing bottleneck.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r9s2 (regression, -7.49%): An EX-only PC-indexed branch predictor avoids fetch-side lookup but still adds BHT read, target-alignment gating, repair redirect state, and update control to the EX redirect/annul cone, so CoreMark's learned branch outcomes did not repay the added control cost.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
