r4s1\tTwo-entry fetch skid buffer\tregression\t-11.89%\t43.842MHz\t4341607\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_hazard > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_if\t49 cells\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r4s1 (regression, -11.89%): A fall-through two-entry fetch skid buffer moves load-use detection onto the buffer head and adds `fetch_consume`/valid/full control in IF, so CoreMark's chance to overlap backend holds with random imem stalls did not repay the extra fetch/decode control cost.\nhyp-20261003-002-r6s1\tForward ready loads into EX\tregression\t-5.56%\t46.407MHz\t4287593\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_mem > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\t17 cells\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r6s1 (regression, -5.56%): Removing the blanket load-use interlock exposed a ready-load bypass from `io_dmemRData` through MEM sign/extend into EX operand forwarding plus `redirect_wait_load` control, and that timing cost outweighed CoreMark's saved dependent-load bubbles.\nhyp-20261003-002-r7s1\tRetire shadow forwarding\tregression\t-10.35%\t46.154MHz\t4491817\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_fwd > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_rf\t38 cells\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r7s1 (regression, -10.35%): Removing `reg_file`'s same-cycle write-first bypass moved RAW repair into retired-write shadow state, but the pending-entry control and wider EX operand forwarding muxes cost more timing than the simpler regfile read path saved.\nhyp-20261003-002-r8s1\tSplit execute address lane\tregression\t-2.46%\t50.213MHz\t4491817\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\t14 cells\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r8s1 (regression, -2.46%): Extending the split rs1+imm lane to LOAD/STORE/JALR still duplicated EX add/control logic without removing the redirect/result-mux dependencies, so address/control specialization remained a timing loss for CoreMark.\nhyp-20261003-002-r9s1\tValid-only pipeline bubbles\tregression\t-5.92%\t48.436MHz\t4491817\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_id/u_decoder > cpu/u_id\t46 cells\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r9s1 (regression, -5.92%): Letting invalid pipeline payloads retain stale values forced extra `valid` gating on load-use, forwarding, redirect, and dmem side-effect paths, so reducing blanket ID/EX/EX/MEM/MEM/WB clears did not relieve CoreMark's dominant control-routing bottleneck.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r2s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r4s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r9s1\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nMOTIVATION:\\\\n\\\\(.motivation)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\\nERROR:\\\\n\\\\(.error // \\\"\\\")\\\\n---\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r2s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r4s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r9s1\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nMOTIVATION:\\\\n\\\\(.motivation)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\\nERROR:\\\\n\\\\(.error // \\\"\\\")\\\\n---\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "ID: hyp-20261003-002-r2s1\nTITLE: Register the IF ID boundary\nMOTIVATION:\nAfter DIV and REM were moved out of the one-cycle ALU, the best timing path\nshifted away from RV32M arithmetic. The current worst path starts in the\nregfile/ID area, passes through EX redirect generation, IF flush/NOP muxing,\nhazard control, and returns to the ID/EX register controls. That shape is a\nconsequence of the current unusual fetch/decode boundary: IF/ID is a\ncombinational bundle, so same-cycle branch, imem-ready, load-use, and decode\ndecisions all sit in one broad feedback cone. A structural retime that adds a\nreal IF/ID register should trade a small amount of control-hazard latency for\na much shorter FPGA clock path.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\nConvert the fetch/decode boundary into a conventional registered IF/ID stage.\nThe IF stage should hold a fetched instruction, PC, and valid bit in flops,\nupdate that register only when decode can accept a new instruction, and flush\nit on redirects. The ID/EX register then consumes the registered IF/ID payload\ninstead of the live imem_data/redirect mux output, while the hazard unit is\nretimed so imem stalls hold IF/ID and decode stalls no longer create a\ncombinational loop through EX redirect and IF flush. Existing single-channel\nRVFI ordering, load-use bubbles, dmem whole-pipeline holds, divider busy\nholds, and misaligned/trap behavior remain unchanged. The expected result is a\nhigher median Fmax by cutting the current ID-EX-IF-hazard-ID feedback path;\nCoreMark may pay an extra bubble on taken redirects, but the present path is\ntiming-dominant enough that a substantial Fmax gain should outweigh the IPC\nloss.\n\nNOTES:\n\nERROR:\n\n---\nID: hyp-20261003-002-r4s1\nTITLE: Two-entry fetch skid buffer\nMOTIVATION:\nThe current champion has already removed the dominant divider path and the\nfailed IF/ID and predictor attempts show that adding latency or fetch-side\ntarget logic is too expensive. However, the benchmark still drives about 22%\nrandom instruction-bus stalls, and the existing front end can only fetch the\ninstru\n…[3271 of 6343 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b75f69a36fd8e4de]…\nlears and enables add control\nfanout to the same area that has become the Fmax limiter.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\nRestructure the pipeline registers so the valid bit and side-effecting control\nfields are the authoritative bubble state, while datapath payload fields are\nallowed to keep old or newly captured values when valid is zero. On reset,\nflush, and EX bubble cycles, clear valid plus the control bits that can write a\nregister, issue memory traffic, redirect fetch, or report a trap, but avoid\ndriving zero muxes across rs values, immediates, PCs, instruction words, and\nRVFI-only payload. Keep the existing MEM/WB hold behavior for dmem stalls so\nload data remains available for forwarding while retirement valid is cleared.\n\nThis should preserve CoreMark cycles while reducing reset/flush/stall control\nfanout and packed-struct muxing in the ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB registers.\nIt targets the post-champion timing shape without adding predictor state,\nextra branch latency, or new memory-side arbitration.\n\nNOTES:\n\nERROR:\n\n---\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
