{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"ls -l cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r10s1.yaml && sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r10s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"-rw-rw----+ 1 hwebench3 hwebench3 3204 Oct  3 20:41 cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r10s1.yaml\nid: hyp-20261003-002-r10s1\ntitle: \"Register the forwarding plan\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion is no longer dominated by division, and most later\n  attempts that added fetch prediction, buffering, or extra execute lanes made\n  the sensitive control routing worse. Several timing reports still show the\n  live MEM/WB or EX/MEM rd/control bits feeding forward_unit, then the EX\n  operand mux, ALU, and dmem-facing result path. That means a small producer\n  tag compare can sit on the same cycle as the 32-bit operand mux and ALU\n  work, even though the needed producer age is already knowable when the\n  consumer instruction is accepted into ID/EX.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move the forwarding priority decision one stage earlier and carry registered\n  forwarding selects with the ID/EX instruction. When ID accepts a fetched\n  instruction, compare its raw rs1/rs2 fields against the current ID/EX writer\n  that will become next cycle's EX/MEM producer and the current EX/MEM writer\n  that will become next cycle's MEM/WB producer. Give the younger producer\n  priority, gate x0 and invalid/non-writing entries, and rely on the existing\n  load-use interlock so load results are selected from MEM/WB only after the\n  bubble. EX then uses these registered select bits directly for the operand\n  muxes instead of recomputing rd/rs equality in the ALU cycle.\n\n  This is a structural retime of the bypass network, not a new predictor or\n  latency trade. It should preserve CoreMark cycles, the one-cycle DSP multiply\n  path, dmem stall semantics, divider holds, and the nret=1 RVFI contract,\n  while removing forward_unit compare and priority logic from the current\n  RF/MEM-WB-to-EX-to-dmem timing cone. The main risk is held ID/EX entries\n  during dmem stalls, so the implementation should either hold the registered\n  selects with the instruction or recompute only on the accept edge; the\n  current MEM/WB hold behavior already keeps selected load data alive for\n  stalled dependents.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add compact registered forwarding-select fields to id_ex_t, keeping the top-level nret=1 IO contract unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Capture the precomputed rs1/rs2 forwarding plan only when ID accepts a new instruction, and flush or hold it with the rest of ID/EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Retarget the forwarding helper to compute next-cycle forwarding selects from raw fetch rs fields, current ID/EX producer metadata, and current EX/MEM producer metadata.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Use the registered ID/EX forwarding selects directly in the operand muxes, removing same-cycle rd/rs compare priority logic from EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the precompute inputs and registered select outputs through the existing decode, hazard, and execute structure without changing external ports.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add focused RAW dependency tests across ALU, load-use, dmem-stall, and divider-hold cases to prove the registered forwarding plan does not select stale producers.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
