{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"structural\\\") | [.id, .title, .outcome, (.delta_pct|tostring), (.fmax_mhz|tostring), (.logic_levels|tostring), (.critical_path.modules|join(\\\">\\\")), .lesson] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "jq: error (at cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:15): Cannot iterate over null (null)\nhyp-20261003-002-r1s1\tSplit RV32M out of the EX critical path\tregression\t696.04\t45.161\t23\tclock_ibuf>cpu/u_id>cpu/u_ex>cpu/u_if>cpu/u_hazard>cpu/u_id\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r1s1 (regression, +696.04%): After DIV/REM are already off the EX critical path, moving MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU behind the same multi-cycle full-pipeline stall trades away CoreMark's useful single-cycle/DSP multiply throughput for little extra timing relief.\nhyp-20261003-002-r2s1\tRegister the IF ID boundary\tregression\t-4.76\t50.363\t14\tclock_ibuf>cpu/u_fwd>cpu/u_ex>cpu/u_ex/u_alu>cpu/u_ex\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, -4.76%): A full registered IF/ID boundary added redirect/imem bubble latency without enough extra relief on the regfile/ID-to-EX-redirect hazard/control path after raw-rs load-use gating, so CoreMark favored the lighter combinational fetch/decode boundary.\nhyp-20261003-002-r3s1\tLate kill redirected fetch\tregression\t-3.55\t49.654\t15\tclock_ibuf>cpu/u_fwd>cpu/u_ex>cpu/u_ex/u_alu>cpu/u_ex\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (regression, -3.55%): Late-killing redirects only through `fetch_kill_for_hazard` and `flush_id` left redirect's flush fanout in the ID/EX control path while exposing wrong-path `imem_data` to decode/regfile addresses, so removing redirect from IF's instruction mux did not improve CoreMark timing.\nhyp-20261003-002-r4s1\tTwo-entry fetch skid buffer\tregression\t-11.89\t43.842\t24\tclock_ibuf>cpu/u_id>cpu/u_ex>cpu/u_if>cpu/u_hazard>cpu/u_id>cpu/u_if\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r4s1 (regression, -11.89%): A fall-through two-entry fetch skid buffer moves load-use detection onto the buffer head and adds `fetch_consume`/valid/full control in IF, so CoreMark's chance to overlap backend holds with random imem stalls did not repay the extra fetch/decode control cost.\nhyp-20261003-002-r6s1\tForward ready loads into EX\tregression\t-5.56\t46.407\t17\tclock_ibuf>cpu/u_mem>cpu/u_ex>cpu/u_ex/u_alu>cpu/u_ex\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r6s1 (regression, -5.56%): Removing the blanket load-use interlock exposed a ready-load bypass from `io_dmemRData` through MEM sign/extend into EX operand forwarding plus `redirect_wait_load` control, and that timing cost outweighed CoreMark's saved dependent-load bubbles.\nhyp-20261003-002-r7s1\tRetire shadow forwarding\tregression\t-10.35\t46.154\t24\tclock_ibuf>cpu/u_fwd>cpu/u_ex>cpu/u_if>cpu/u_rf\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r7s1 (regression, -10.35%): Removing `reg_file`'s same-cycle write-first bypass moved RAW repair into retired-write shadow state, but the pending-entry control and wider EX operand forwarding muxes cost more timing than the simpler regfile read path saved.\nhyp-20261003-002-r8s1\tSplit execute address lane\tregression\t-2.46\t50.213\t14\tclock_ibuf>cpu/u_id>cpu/u_ex>cpu/u_ex/u_alu>cpu/u_ex\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r8s1 (regression, -2.46%): Extending the split rs1+imm lane to LOAD/STORE/JALR still duplicated EX add/control logic without removing the redirect/result-mux dependencies, so address/control specialization remained a timing loss for CoreMark.\nhyp-20261003-002-r9s1\tValid-only pipeline bubbles\tregression\t-5.92\t48.436\t23\tclock_ibuf>cpu/u_id>cpu/u_ex>cpu/u_if>cpu/u_id/u_decoder>cpu/u_id\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r9s1 (regression, -5.92%): Letting invalid pipeline payloads retain stale values forced extra `valid` gating on load-use, forwarding, redirect, and dmem side-effect paths, so reducing blanket ID/EX/EX/MEM/MEM/WB clears did not relieve CoreMark's dominant control-routing bottleneck.\nhyp-20261003-002-r10s1\tRegister the forwarding plan\timprovement\t4.92\t54.016\t12\tclock_ibuf>cpu/u_id>cpu/u_ex>cpu/u_ex/u_alu>cpu/u_ex\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r10s1 (improvement, +4.92%): Registering ID/EX `fwd_rs1_sel`/`fwd_rs2_sel` on instruction accept paid because producer priority was already knowable from current ID/EX and EX/MEM, removing rd/rs compares from the EX operand-mux/ALU/dmem cone while the load-use interlock preserves load timing.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
