{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r10s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-001-r10s0\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r11s1\\\") | .id+\\\"\\\\nTITLE: \\\"+.title+\\\"\\\\nCATEGORY: \\\"+.category+\\\"\\\\nOUTCOME: \\\"+.outcome+\\\" delta=\\\"+(.delta_pct|tostring)+\\\" fmax=\\\"+(.fmax_mhz|tostring)+\\\"\\\\nHYP: \\\"+(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis | tostring)+\\\"\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261003-001-r10s0\nTITLE: Offload high-half multiplies\nCATEGORY: micro_opt\nOUTCOME: regression delta=0.65 fmax=51.818\nHYP: Keep ALU_MUL as a single-cycle DSP-backed low-half multiply, but remove\nMULH, MULHU, and MULHSU from the common combinational ALU result path. Handle\nthose high-half multiply opcodes with a small multi-cycle execute-side unit\nthat captures operands, computes the signed/unsigned high half off the normal\nALU mux, and reuses the existing divider-style pipeline hold until the result\nis ready. DIV/REM behavior, ALTOPS substitutions, branch timing, load/store\ntiming, and the top-level nret=1 contract stay unchanged.\n\nThis avoids the failed all-MUL multi-cycle tradeoff by preserving CoreMark's\nuseful single-cycle MUL throughput, and avoids the failed combinational shared\nhigh-multiply correction path by not putting sign-correction carry chains after\nthe DSP output on every cycle. If CoreMark rarely executes high-half multiply\ninstructions, IPC should remain effectively flat while the reduced DSP/mux\npressure in the EX ALU cone improves median Tang Nano 20K Fmax.\n\n\nhyp-20261003-002-r10s1\nTITLE: Register the forwarding plan\nCATEGORY: structural\nOUTCOME: improvement delta=4.92 fmax=54.016\nHYP: Move the forwarding priority decision one stage earlier and carry registered\nforwarding selects with the ID/EX instruction. When ID accepts a fetched\ninstruction, compare its raw rs1/rs2 fields against the current ID/EX writer\nthat will become next cycle's EX/MEM producer and the current EX/MEM writer\nthat will become next cycle's MEM/WB producer. Give the younger producer\npriority, gate x0 and invalid/non-writing entries, and rely on the existing\nload-use interlock so load results are selected from MEM/WB only after the\nbubble. EX then uses these registered select bits directly for the operand\nmuxes instead of recomputing rd/rs equality in the ALU cycle.\n\nThis is a structural retime of the bypass network, not a new predictor or\nlatency trade. It should preserve CoreMark cycles, the one-cycle DSP multiply\npath, dmem stall semantics, divider holds, and the nret=1 RVFI contract,\nwhile removing forward_unit compare and priority logic from the current\nRF/MEM-WB-to-EX-to-dmem timing cone. The main risk is held ID/EX entries\nduring dmem stalls, so the implementation should either hold the registered\nselects with the instruction or recompute only on the accept edge; the\ncurrent MEM/WB hold behavior already keeps selected load data alive for\nstalled dependents.\n\n\nhyp-20261003-002-r11s1\nTITLE: Compact registered writeback slice\nCATEGORY: structural\nOUTCOME: regression delta=-6.45 fmax=50.528\nHYP: Collapse MEM/WB into a compact registered writeback slice: latch final\nwb_data, wb_en, rd, trap, and the RVFI memory metadata at the MEM/WB\nboundary instead of carrying the full ctrl_t plus separate alu_result and\nread_data values into a late WB mux. MEM should compute the sign/zero\nextended load value as it already does, choose the final writeback data at\nthe MEM/WB boundary, and retain that registered value across dmem hold\ncycles exactly as it currently retains load forwarding fields. The wb_stage\nthen becomes only valid/wb_en/rd gating, and EX's registered forwarding\nselect for FWD_MEM_WB points at a flop output rather than a 32-bit\npost-register mux.\n\nThis is a narrow pipeline-structure retime, not a predictor or latency\ntrade. It should preserve the current one-cycle ALU and MUL paths,\nload-use interlock behavior, RVFI retirement fields, and the nret=1\ntop-level contract while shaving another mux level and some routing from\nthe WB-to-ID/EX bypass cone. The main risk is dmem-stall forwarding, so the\nimplementation must hold the registered wb_data, rd, and compact writeback\ncontrol when MEM/WB valid is cleared during a memory wait.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
