{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r13s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r13s1\ntitle: \"Operand-capture execute retime\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion is no longer limited by the old combinational divider;\n  recent timing reports keep landing on short but stubborn paths from the\n  ID/EX operand side through EX forwarding, the ALU, and either dmem-facing\n  result routing or the redirect/control loop. Prior predictor, fetch-buffer,\n  decode-target, writeback, and narrow fast-add attempts mostly lost because\n  they added routing without cutting this path at a real register boundary.\n  A structural execute retime is the remaining high-upside lever: shorten the\n  common EX launch-to-capture path even if it costs one more control-flow\n  recovery cycle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split the current EX stage into an operand-capture stage and an arithmetic\n  stage. The first stage owns the existing EX forwarding muxes, AUIPC/JALR\n  operand selection, store-data capture, and DIV/REM start/hold bookkeeping;\n  it latches resolved operands and the decoded control into a new EX1/EX2\n  bundle. The second stage runs the existing ALU, branch comparator, jump\n  target calculation, single-cycle MUL path, DIV/REM result handoff, and\n  EX/MEM register write from those captured operands.\n\n  Keep ALU and MUL latency at one cycle relative to the new EX2 stage and\n  forward producer results into the operand-capture stage from EX2, EX/MEM,\n  and MEM/WB so ordinary ALU dependency chains do not gain a blanket stall.\n  Loads should still use the existing load-use interlock, with distances\n  updated for the extra execute boundary. Branches and jumps resolve one stage\n  later, so CoreMark will pay some extra redirect bubbles, but the expected\n  Fmax gain comes from removing the forwarding mux, regfile routing, and broad\n  ID/EX fanout from the same timed cone as the 32-bit ALU and dmem address\n  path. This is deliberately a pipeline-boundary change rather than another\n  predictor or sideband adder, and it preserves RVFI order, trap semantics,\n  memory alignment checks, and the nret=1 top-level interface.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add an EX1/EX2 pipeline bundle carrying captured operands, store data, PC, immediate, control, and RVFI source metadata while keeping nret=1 unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Restructure execute into operand-capture and arithmetic sub-stages, with ALU/branch/DIV handoff driven from registered captured operands.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Update forwarding priority and source selection so operands captured in EX1 can see results from EX2, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB without a blanket ALU-use stall.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Retarget load-use, divider-busy, dmem-stall, and redirect flush/hold controls for the extra execute boundary and the later branch resolution point.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the new execute-stage boundary and forwarding sources while preserving all top-level imem, dmem, and RVFI ports.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add focused dependency, branch-redirect, load-use, dmem-stall, MUL, and DIV/REM tests that exercise the deeper execute pipeline.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
