{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r15s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r15s1\ntitle: \"Selective operand latch for memory ops\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion's worst paths repeatedly run from the regfile/ID side\n  through EX operand selection, the ALU, and the dmem-facing EX/MEM result.\n  Prior full execute retimes had enough upside to revisit, but they broke\n  because every instruction was pushed through a deeper execute pipe without\n  extending load-use handling far enough. A narrower structural split can\n  target the CoreMark memory-address cone while avoiding a blanket branch and\n  ALU latency increase.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a small pre-address latch used only by load and store instructions.\n  Decode/ID keeps the normal five-stage path for ALU, branch, jump, and MUL\n  operations, preserving their current bypass and redirect timing. When a\n  memory instruction enters EX, an EX1 register captures the already-forwarded\n  base register, store data, immediate, rd/rs metadata, PC, instruction, and\n  memory control; the following cycle computes the effective address and\n  advances into the existing MEM stage. While this one-entry memory pipe is\n  occupied, younger instructions are held at ID/EX unless they are proven\n  independent by the existing simple rules.\n\n  This is a structural retime, not a memory predictor: loads and stores still\n  retire in order, use the same dmem ready protocol, and report identical RVFI\n  fields. The key difference from the broken full execute split is that\n  non-memory ALU chains and branches do not gain an extra stage, and load\n  dependences are handled conservatively by stalling consumers until the load\n  reaches MEM/WB. The expected benefit is a shorter FPGA critical path for\n  CoreMark's frequent load/store address generation because