{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_17", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nids={'hyp-20261003-002-r10s1','hyp-20261003-002-r12s1','hyp-20261003-001-r13s0','hyp-20261003-002-r13s1','hyp-20261003-002-r14s1'}\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    if not line.strip(): continue\n    o=json.loads(line)\n    if o.get('id') in ids:\n        print('='*80)\n        print(o['id'], o.get('title'), o.get('category'), o.get('outcome'), o.get('fitness'), o.get('fmax_mhz'), o.get('delta_pct'))\n        print('motivation:\\\\n', o.get('motivation','')[:2000])\n        h=o.get('hypothesis')\n        if isinstance(h,dict): h=h.get('hypothesis','')\n        print('hypothesis:\\\\n', h[:3000] if h else '')\n        print('notes:\\\\n', (o.get('implementation_notes') or '')[:3000])\n        print('error:', o.get('error'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "================================================================================\nhyp-20261003-002-r10s1 Register the forwarding plan structural improvement 120.25 54.016 4.92\nmotivation:\n The current champion is no longer dominated by division, and most later\nattempts that added fetch prediction, buffering, or extra execute lanes made\nthe sensitive control routing worse. Several timing reports still show the\nlive MEM/WB or EX/MEM rd/control bits feeding forward_unit, then the EX\noperand mux, ALU, and dmem-facing result path. That means a small producer\ntag compare can sit on the same cycle as the 32-bit operand mux and ALU\nwork, even though the needed producer age is already knowable when the\nconsumer instruction is accepted into ID/EX.\n\nhypothesis:\n Move the forwarding priority decision one stage earlier and carry registered\nforwarding selects with the ID/EX instruction. When ID accepts a fetched\ninstruction, compare its raw rs1/rs2 fields against the current ID/EX writer\nthat will become next cycle's EX/MEM producer and the current EX/MEM writer\nthat will become next cycle's MEM/WB producer. Give the younger producer\npriority, gate x0 and invalid/non-writing entries, and rely on the existing\nload-use interlock so load results are selected from MEM/WB only after the\nbubble. EX then uses these registered select bits directly for the operand\nmuxes instead of recomputing rd/rs equality in the ALU cycle.\n\nThis is a structural retime of the bypass network, not a new predictor or\nlatency trade. It should preserve CoreMark cycles, the one-cycle DSP multiply\npath, dmem stall semantics, divider holds, and the nret=1 RVFI contract,\nwhile removing forward_unit compare and priority logic from the current\nRF/MEM-WB-to-EX-to-dmem timing cone. The main risk is held ID/EX entries\nduring dmem stalls, so the implementation should either hold the registered\nselects with the instruction or recompute only on the accept edge; the\ncurrent MEM/WB hold behavior already keeps selected load data alive for\nstalled dependents.\n\nnotes:\n \nerr\n…[6801 of 9873 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7c11d6e9d6f50b8a]…\n\nthe current ALU, branch comparator, jump target calculation, single-cycle\nMUL path, DIV/REM result handoff, and EX/MEM register write from those\ncaptured operands.\n\nCorrectness is preserved by tracking load-producing rd values through the\nnew EX boundary and EX/MEM. A consumer is held in ID/EX until the load can\nbe forwarded from MEM/WB, or, if an implementation chooses the faster but\nriskier option, until an exact MEM-stage load-data bypass is available.\nThis directly fixes the r13s1 failure mode where one-gap load consumers\nadvanced too early. Ordinary ALU chains should still forward from the\narithmetic stage, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB into operand capture so they do not\ngain a blanket extra stall. Branches resolve one stage later and some load\ndependences may pay another bubble, but the expected Fmax win comes from\nremoving forwarding muxes, raw regfile routing, and broad ID/EX fanout from\nthe same timed cone as the 32-bit ALU and dmem address path.\n\nnotes:\n \nerror: cosim_failed: random2.elf [--istall --dstall]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
