{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r15s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r15s1\ntitle: \"Exclusive memory address lane\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion's worst paths repeatedly run from the regfile/ID side\n  through EX operand selection, the ALU, and the dmem-facing EX/MEM result.\n  A prior structural memory-address sideband was a useful near miss: it raised\n  Fmax, but the lesson was that the old ALU-result dependency still remained\n  in the critical cone. The next step is to make the address/result split\n  exclusive, so load/store addresses no longer fan out through the same\n  register and mux network used for writeback, jumps, shifts, compares, and\n  multiply results.\nhypothesis: |\n  Restructure the EX/MEM bundle into two mutually exclusive datapath lanes:\n  a general writeback/result lane for non-memory instructions, and a dedicated\n  effective-address lane for LOAD/STORE. Expose the raw ADD sum from the ALU\n  or compute it as an address-only signal before the final ALU result mux, then\n  latch it into a new EX/MEM mem_addr field whenever mem_read or mem_write is\n  set. For memory instructions, keep the general alu_result field benign and\n  unused for forwarding/writeback, and make mem_stage, misalignment checks,\n  dmem_addr, and RVFI memory address fields consume only the dedicated mem_addr\n  lane.\n\n  This keeps the five-stage timing and CoreMark cycle count unchanged: no new\n  predictor, no posted memory behavior, and no extra load/store stage. It also\n  avoids the broken full execute-retime failure mode because load-use timing\n  and MEM/WB forwarding remain exactly where they are today. The expected win\n  is a routing and mux-depth reduction on the hot dmem path: the address adder\n  still exists, but its registered output no longer has to be the same\n  high-fanout alu_result value that feeds the writeback path and EX/MEM\n  forwarding eligibility. If the old alu_result dependency is truly removed\n  for memory ops, this should improve on the previous sideband near-miss\n  enough to clear the acceptance threshold.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Expose the ADD carry-chain result as a sideband before the final ALU result mux, or otherwise provide an address-only sum without duplicating a second full adder.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add a dedicated EX/MEM mem_addr field and document that alu_result is not the architectural memory address lane for LOAD/STORE.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch memory effective addresses into the dedicated mem_addr lane and keep alu_result benign for memory ops so the old result fanout is removed from the dmem cone.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Drive dmem_addr, misalignment detection, byte masks, load extraction shifts, and RVFI mem_addr from EX/MEM mem_addr instead of alu_result.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Update forwarding-eligibility misalignment checks to use the dedicated memory address lane while preserving the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_mem_addr_lane.py\n    description: \"Add focused cocotb coverage for load/store addresses, misaligned traps, load-use forwarding, store data forwarding, and RVFI memory fields after the lane split.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
