{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r5s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\ntitle: \"Post data stores past dmem stalls\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion has already learned the hard lessons: fetch-side\n  predictors and skid buffers add too much control timing, and moving multiply\n  off the single-cycle path costs useful CoreMark throughput. A remaining\n  structural opportunity is the dmem side. Today any stalled store in MEM\n  freezes the whole pipeline, even though an ordinary data-memory store has no\n  register result and many following ALU or branch instructions could execute\n  while the external write waits for the next ready pulse.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a one-entry posted store buffer between MEM and the dmem write port for\n  bufferable data-RAM stores only. When a store to the normal memory window\n  reaches MEM and io_dmemReady is low, enqueue its address, replicated write\n  data, byte mask, and RVFI memory metadata, let that store retire through the\n  existing MEM/WB boundary, and keep driving the buffered write on the dmem\n  port until it is accepted. Loads, later stores, and all MMIO/marker writes\n  remain blocking while the buffer is occupied, so memory ordering, UART\n  output, and the CoreMark start/stop markers stay precise.\n\n  This should recover IPC from the roughly 22 percent random dmem stall stream\n  by hiding store wait cycles under independent non-memory instructions. The\n  added state is only one narrow queue entry and a dmem-output mux in MEM,\n  keeping the previously fragile IF redirect, fetch prediction, and RV32M ALU\n  timing paths essentially unchanged.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/store_buffer.sv\n    description: \"Add a one-entry posted store queue that captures bufferable data-RAM stores when dmem is not ready and drains them in order.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Retire enqueued stores through MEM/WB, mux buffered writes onto the dmem port, and keep MMIO/marker stores and misaligned accesses unbuffered.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Replace the blanket dmem-store freeze with stalls only for loads, non-bufferable stores, or any memory op that would conflict with an occupied store buffer.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire store-buffer full/drain status between MEM and hazard control while preserving the existing nret=1 IO and RVFI retirement contract.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add any small control bit needed to identify bufferable data-RAM stores without widening unrelated pipeline paths.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
