{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"structural\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct//\\\"n/a\\\"),(.fmax_mhz//\\\"n/a\\\"),(.cycles//\\\"n/a\\\"),(.critical_path.modules//[]|join(\\\" > \\\")),((.critical_path.logic_cells//\\\"n/a\\\")|tostring),(.lesson//\\\"\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20261003-002-r1s1\tSplit RV32M out of the EX critical path\tregression\t696.04\t45.161\t4680666\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_hazard > cpu/u_id\t52\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r1s1 (regression, +696.04%): After DIV/REM are already off the EX critical path, moving MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU behind the same multi-cycle full-pipeline stall trades away CoreMark's useful single-cycle/DSP multiply throughput for little extra timing relief.\nhyp-20261003-002-r2s1\tRegister the IF ID boundary\tregression\t-4.76\t50.363\t4949188\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_fwd > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\t14\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, -4.76%): A full registered IF/ID boundary added redirect/imem bubble latency without enough extra relief on the regfile/ID-to-EX-redirect hazard/control path after raw-rs load-use gating, so CoreMark favored the lighter combinational fetch/decode boundary.\nhyp-20261003-002-r3s1\tLate kill redirected fetch\tregression\t-3.55\t49.654\t4491817\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_fwd > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\t16\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (regression, -3.55%): Late-killing redirects only through `fetch_kill_for_hazard` and `flush_id` left redirect's flush fanout in the ID/EX control path while exposing wrong-path `imem_data` to decode/regfile addresses, so removing redirect from IF's instruction mux did not improve CoreMark timing.\nhyp-20261003-002-r4s1\tTwo-entry fetch skid buffer\tregression\t-11.89\t43.842\t4341607\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_hazard > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_if\t49\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r4s1 (regression, -11.89%): A fall-through two-entry fetch skid buffer moves load-use detection onto the buffer head and adds `fetch_consume`/valid/full control in IF, so CoreMark's chance to overlap backend holds with random imem stalls did not repay the extra fetch/decode control cost.\nhyp-20261003-002-r5s1\tPost data stores past dmem stalls\tplacement_failed\tn/a\tn/a\tn/a\t\tn/a\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r5s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Posting dmem stores by muxing a buffered writer onto the byte-lane write port can break Gowin BRAM inference for the FPGA wrapper's 8 KiB dmem, falling back to per-lane DFF memory and exhausting FF resources before timing is measured.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
