{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r6s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Forward ready loads into EX\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion keeps the simple five-stage front end that has timed best so far, but it still pays a fixed one-cycle load-use bubble before every immediately dependent consumer. CoreMark's hot code is pointer and array heavy, and the external dmem stall stream already handles the real wait when a load is not ready. If the load reaches MEM on a ready cycle, the dependent instruction can use the returned data directly instead of sitting behind an unconditional bubble.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a MEM-to-EX forwarding path for aligned loads whose dmem response is ready in the same cycle. Remove the blanket load-use interlock from the hazard unit, let the dependent instruction enter EX immediately behind the load, and forward the MEM-stage sign/zero-extended load result into the existing operand muxes with priority over older MEM/WB data. When io_dmemReady is low, the existing dmem stall still holds EX/MEM and ID/EX until the response is valid; when the load is misaligned or traps, the new forward-valid signal stays low so the younger instruction observes the old architectural register value.\n\n  This is a structural latency reduction rather than a fetch predictor: it should improve iterations per cycle by removing many ready-load bubbles while avoiding the tag/update/control feedback paths that hurt prior predictor and skid-buffer attempts. The Fmax risk is the added dmemRData-to-EX bypass mux, but that path is only a narrow selected load-data path and should be smaller than the failed front-end structures.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Expose a MEM-stage load-forward data value and valid bit after width extraction, sign extension, alignment checking, and dmem-ready qualification.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Treat an EX/MEM load as a forwardable producer only when the MEM-stage load-forward valid signal is high, while preserving ALU-result and MEM/WB priority behavior.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Select the forwarded MEM load result through the existing rs1/rs2 operand muxes so dependent ALU, branch, jump, and store-data consumers can execute without a fixed bubble.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Remove the unconditional load-use stall and rely on the existing dmem stall to hold the load and its dependent consumer only when the load response is actually not ready.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the MEM-stage load-forward data and valid signals into forwarding and execute without changing the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add or adjust focused pipeline tests for load-use consumers with ready loads, stalled loads, store-data dependencies, and misaligned load traps.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
