{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')\nfor line in p.read_text().splitlines():\n    j=json.loads(line)\n    if j.get('id') in {'hyp-20261003-002-r2s1','hyp-20261003-002-r4s1','hyp-20261003-002-r6s1'}:\n        print('---', j['id'], j['title'], j['outcome'], j['delta_pct'])\n        print('motivation:', (j.get('motivation') or '')[:1000])\n        h=j.get('hypothesis')\n        if isinstance(h, dict): h=h.get('hypothesis')\n        print('hypothesis:', (h or '')[:1500])\n        print('critical:', j.get('critical_path'))\n        print('notes:', (j.get('implementation_notes') or '')[:1200])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "--- hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 Register the IF ID boundary regression -4.76\nmotivation: After DIV and REM were moved out of the one-cycle ALU, the best timing path\nshifted away from RV32M arithmetic. The current worst path starts in the\nregfile/ID area, passes through EX redirect generation, IF flush/NOP muxing,\nhazard control, and returns to the ID/EX register controls. That shape is a\nconsequence of the current unusual fetch/decode boundary: IF/ID is a\ncombinational bundle, so same-cycle branch, imem-ready, load-use, and decode\ndecisions all sit in one broad feedback cone. A structural retime that adds a\nreal IF/ID register should trade a small amount of control-hazard latency for\na much shorter FPGA clock path.\n\nhypothesis: Convert the fetch/decode boundary into a conventional registered IF/ID stage.\nThe IF stage should hold a fetched instruction, PC, and valid bit in flops,\nupdate that register only when decode can accept a new instruction, and flush\nit on redirects. The ID/EX register then consumes the registered IF/ID payload\ninstead of the live imem_data/redirect mux output, while the hazard unit is\nretimed so imem stalls hold IF/ID and decode stalls no longer create a\ncombinational loop through EX redirect and IF flush. Existing single-channel\nRVFI ordering, load-use bubbles, dmem whole-pipeline holds, divider busy\nholds, and misaligned/trap behavior remain unchanged. The expected result is a\nhigher median Fmax by cutting the current ID-EX-IF-hazard-ID feedback path;\nCoreMark may pay an extra bubble on taken redirects, but the present path is\ntiming-dominant enough that a substantial Fmax gain should outweigh the IPC\nloss.\n\ncritical: {'from': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0', 'to': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -15.05, 'arrival_ns': 25.345, 'logic_cells': 14, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nnotes: \n--- hyp-20261003-002-r4s1 Two-entry fetch skid buffer regression -11.89\nmotivation: The current champion has already removed the dominant divider path and the\nfailed I\n…[2559 of 5631 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0ccf0558930ce9d5]…\nward the MEM-stage sign/zero-extended load result into the existing operand muxes with priority over older MEM/WB data. When io_dmemReady is low, the existing dmem stall still holds EX/MEM and ID/EX until the response is valid; when the load is misaligned or traps, the new forward-valid signal stays low so the younger instruction observes the old architectural register value.\n\nThis is a structural latency reduction rather than a fetch predictor: it should improve iterations per cycle by removing many ready-load bubbles while avoiding the tag/update/control feedback paths that hurt prior predictor and skid-buffer attempts. The Fmax risk is the added dmemRData-to-EX bypass mux, but that path is only a narrow selected load-data path and should be smaller than the failed front-end structures.\n\ncritical: {'from': 'dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -17.326, 'arrival_ns': 27.621, 'logic_cells': 17, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_mem', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nnotes: \n", "exit