{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r7s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r7s1\ntitle: \"Retire shadow forwarding\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion's worst path is no longer the divider or a fetch predictor path; it runs through the regfile, ID, EX redirect/control, IF, and back into regfile control. The register file still implements write-first same-cycle bypassing from the combinational WB mux into the asynchronous read ports, which makes every ID read depend on the current retirement write path. That bypass exists to cover the case where an instruction in ID reads the register being written in WB, but the value is not actually needed until the consumer reaches EX one cycle later.\nhypothesis: |\n  Remove the write-first bypass from rtl/reg_file.sv so the register array read path is only x0 gating plus the selected stored word. Add a tiny retired-write shadow register in rtl/core.sv that captures wb_w_en, wb_w_addr, and wb_w_data each cycle, then extend rtl/forward_unit.sv and rtl/ex_stage.sv with a lowest-priority WB-shadow forwarding source into the existing EX operand muxes. This preserves RAW correctness for consumers that were relying on same-cycle WB-to-ID visibility, while cutting the current combinational WB/regfile/read/control feedback cone. Because the extra source is registered and older than MEM/WB, it should be cheaper than the current write-first regfile path and may lift Fmax enough to beat the champion without adding branch, load, or memory latency.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Remove the combinational write-first read bypass so rs1_data and rs2_data come only from x0 gating or the stored register word.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Capture each valid WB register write into a one-cycle retired-write shadow and wire it as an additional forwarding candidate.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Add lowest-priority comparisons for the retired-write shadow after EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding so WB-to-ID consumers are repaired in EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Extend the rs1 and rs2 operand muxes with the registered retired-write shadow data while preserving existing forwarding priority.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add a focused RAW test where an instruction in ID reads the same register being written by WB, proving the new shadow forward replaces write-first regfile behavior.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
