 hyp-20261003-001-r3s0 (regression, -1.67%): After raw-rs load-use gating, removing IF's flush-to-NOP instruction mux only moved bubble control into `in.valid` gates on ID regfile addresses, ID/EX zeroing, and EX redirect quali\n…[3214 of 6286 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e1e630a55a57ca60]…\nt enough; the IF countdown/decrement/target-mux state plus ID/EX prediction repair plumbing still cost more timing than CoreMark's hot backedge bubbles saved.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r7s0 (regression, +3.70%): Precomputing `pc_plus4`/`pc_imm_target` in ID regressed because the added ID adders and 64 bits of ID/EX payload sit on the already-sensitive decode/regfile/control routing, while EX redirect still needs branch/JALR mux/control work.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r7s1 (regression, -10.35%): Removing `reg_file`'s same-cycle write-first bypass moved RAW repair into retired-write shadow state, but the pending-entry control and wider EX operand forwarding muxes cost more timing than the simpler regfile read path saved.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r7s2 (regression, -2.48%): Even JAL-only decode prediction puts an immediate/PC target adder and same-cycle ID-to-IF redirect mux/control path on sensitive decode routing, so CoreMark's direct-jump bubble savings did not repay the added predictor plumbing.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
