{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r7s2\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, .changes' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r7s2\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, .changes' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"Add a narrow decode-stage direct-jump predictor for JAL only.  When the\ncurrent fetched instruction is a valid JAL, imem is ready, decode is not\nbeing stalled or flushed, and the computed JAL target is word-aligned, ID\nredirects the fetch PC to pc+imm at the same clock edge that the JAL enters\nID/EX.  Carry a single predicted-direct-jump bit in ID/EX so EX suppresses\nthe normal JAL redirect when the early prediction was used; branches and\nJALR remain on the existing EX-resolution path.\n\nThis should remove one wrong-path fetch bubble from every correctly aligned\ndirect call or jump without a BTB, fetch-side target adder, saturating\ncounter, return stack, or predictor table.  The added logic is a single\nJAL opcode check, reuse of ID's immediate/pc adder, one PC-select input in\nIF, and one suppress bit in EX, so it targets CoreMark call/control-flow\nbubbles while avoiding the predictor structures that hurt Fmax in earlier\nrounds.\n\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\n    \"description\": \"Add a predicted_direct_jump bit to the ID/EX bundle so EX can distinguish a JAL already redirected by decode from ordinary branches and JALR.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\n    \"description\": \"Detect valid aligned JAL instructions when ID is allowed to advance, compute the direct target with the existing immediate path, and emit the early prediction metadata.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\n    \"description\": \"Add a decode-predict redirect input that updates the PC to the direct JAL target without flushing the current instruction being captured.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\n    \"description\": \"Suppress the normal EX-stage JAL redirect when the carried direct-jump prediction is known correct, while keeping misaligned targets on the existing trap path.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n    \"description\": \"Wire the decode-stage JAL prediction target and suppress bit between ID, IF, and EX without changing the top-level IO contract.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\",\n    \"description\": \"Add focused pipeline coverage for an aligned JAL using the decode-stage prediction and for a misaligned JAL target falling back to the existing trap path.\"\n  }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r8s2\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, .changes' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r8s2\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, .changes' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"Replace same-cycle conditional-branch redirect qualification with a static\nbackward-taken/forward-not-taken predictor that is verified one cycle later.\nIn EX, compute the predicted conditional next PC using only the registered\nbranch immediate sign: backward branches redirect to their PC-relative target,\nforward branches fall through. Keep computing the real branch condition for\nRVFI pc_next and correctness, but feed it only into a registered mispredict\nrepair path. On a mismatch, the next cycle asserts the existing redirect/flush\nmachinery toward the real next PC and annuls the wrong-path instruction that\nhas reached EX.\n\nThis keeps BTBs, counters, target tables, JAL predecode, and return-stack\nmatching out of IF. Hot CoreMark loop backedges should behave like the current\ntaken branches, while the same-cycle redirect control no longer depends on\nthe 32-bit rs1/rs2 comparator and forwarding mux outputs. Loop exits and\nforward taken branches pay a delayed repair bubble, but those should be rarer\nthan taken backedges; if the regfile/EX/IF feedback path is the real limiter,\nthe Fmax gain can outweigh the modest IPC loss.\n\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\n    \"description\": \"Drive conditional-branch redirects from a static BTFNT prediction, compute actual branch results off the immediate redirect path, register mispredict repairs, and annul EX output on delayed repair cycles.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n    \"description\": \"Treat delayed repair redirects like ordinary redirects for IF/ID flushing while preserving dmem-stall and divider priority rules.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n    \"description\": \"Wire any added repair or annul control from EX through the existing redirect/flush path without changing the top-level IO contract.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\",\n    \"description\": \"Add focused coverage for backward-taken prediction, backward loop-exit repair, and forward-taken repair so wrong-path instructions cannot retire.\"\n  }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
