{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261003-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny BTB branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current core always fetches fall-through and redirects every taken branch or jump from EX, inserting a bubble on the hot loop branches that CoreMark executes repeatedly. A small predictor should improve iterations/cycle without touching the long ALU/forwarding critical path that currently limits Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core always fetches fall-through and redirects every taken branch or jump from EX, inserting a bubble on the hot loop branches that CoreMark executes repeatedly. A small predictor should improve iterations/cycle without touching the long ALU/forwarding critical path that currently limits Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped branch target buffer with 2-bit saturating counters, indexed by fetch PC and updated when branches or jumps resolve in EX. The IF stage uses a tagged BTB hit with a taken counter to select the predicted next PC, while ID/EX carries that predicted next PC so EX only redirects and flushes on a mismatch between predicted and resolved next PC. Train unconditional JAL/JALR as taken and train conditional branches by actual outcome; wrong predictions still recover through the existing redirect/flush path. This should remove most recurring taken-branch and repeated call/return bubbles in CoreMark for modest LUT/FF cost and limited Fmax risk.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 12, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles so EX can compare prediction against the resolved next PC.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.