{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r13s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-003-r13s2\ntitle: \"Aligned load forwarding predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Prior branch, jump, return, loop, and replay predictors all paid too much\n  timing cost in IF/ID PC-selection or EX repair control for the CoreMark\n  bubbles they removed. The remaining predictor opportunity is a narrower\n  one that does not alter fetch: predict that memory-producing loads are\n  naturally aligned, which is true for CoreMark's hot data accesses and for\n  normal compiler output, and remove that alignment check from the forwarding\n  select path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the exact EX/MEM forwarding eligibility check for load alignment\n  with a speculative aligned-load predictor. The forward unit should treat a\n  valid EX/MEM register-writing instruction as a future MEM/WB forwarding\n  producer without first checking the low address bits for misalignment.\n  Correctness is recovered one stage later in the EX operand mux: when an\n  operand selected FWD_MEM_WB, use the MEM/WB value only if the actual MEM/WB\n  control still says the producer writes rd. Misaligned loads already clear\n  reg_write in mem_stage when they become trapping instructions, so their\n  consumers fall back to the original ID/EX regfile operand instead of seeing\n  bogus load data.\n\n  This is a predictor in the narrow sense: the common case predicts aligned\n  load completion and keeps the rd/rs forwarding decision shallow, while the\n  rare misaligned trap is validated late with no fetch predictor, target\n  adder, branch table, replay buffer, or PC mux. The expected win is a small\n  but useful Fmax lift on the ID/EX-to-forward-to-EX cone with unchanged\n  CoreMark cycles, unchanged memory protocol, and no weakening of RVFI trap\n  behavior for misaligned accesses.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Remove the EX/MEM low-bit misalignment check from forwarding eligibility and wire a late MEM/WB write-valid indication into EX for operand validation.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Gate FWD_MEM_WB operand use with the actual MEM/WB reg_write/rd-valid state, falling back to the captured ID/EX operand when the predicted producer became a trapping no-write load.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Preserve the existing misaligned-load trap behavior that clears reg_write before MEM/WB so the late forwarding validator has an exact no-write signal.\"\n  - file: test/test_misaligned_forward.py\n    description: \"Add focused cocotb coverage for a misaligned load followed by a dependent ALU or branch instruction, plus an aligned load case that must still forward normally.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
