{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r15s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-003-r15s2\ntitle: \"Phase-locked imem stall prefetch\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Prior predictor attempts that selected branch targets, replayed loops, or posted stores lost too much timing or disturbed memory inference. The benchmark still pays many cycles to the deterministic instruction-ready stall stream, and the fixture documents the exact xorshift phase used by both cosim and FPGA timing. A predictor that uses that phase only as a validated hint can target IPC without adding BTB/tag/target logic to the sensitive branch PC-select path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a phase-locked instruction-stall predictor in the fetch stage, mirroring the documented xorshift ready generator to know when the next instruction-side ready bubble is likely. Use the prediction to run a one-entry sequential prefetch/replay buffer: when the fall-through path is linear and the buffer can be filled on a real ready cycle, keep the next PC/data pair available so a later predicted imem bubble can be absorbed without presenting an invalid instruction to ID. The external io_imemReady pin remains authoritative; on any mismatch, redirect, non-linear PC change, or buffer uncertainty, fall back to the current direct-fetch behavior and invalidate the buffer.\n\n  The key distinction from earlier branch and replay predictors is that no branch target lookup, immediate adder, or predictor tag compare sits on the IF PC-select path. The predictor state advances from reset in registered form, the buffer output carries pre-decoded raw rs fields so load-use and forwarding keep their short registered-select structure, and redirects simply clear the buffer. If the deterministic stall phase matches, CoreMark should recover a meaningful fraction of instruction-stall bubbles; if it does not, correctness is preserved by the existing handshake and flush rules.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add a phase-locked imem-ready predictor and a one-entry validated sequential prefetch/replay buffer that is cleared on redirects and falls back to direct fetch on real-ready mismatch.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Extend the IF/ID fetch bundle with buffered raw source fields or a buffer-valid marker so downstream hazard logic need not decode through an added fetch mux.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the buffered fetch metadata into load-use and forwarding precompute while preserving the existing nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Treat a validated buffered instruction exactly like a ready imem fetch and keep redirect, dmem stall, and divider stall priorities unchanged.\"\n  - file: test/test_imem_ready_predictor.py\n    description: \"Add cocotb coverage for predicted imem stalls, predictor mismatch fallback, redirect buffer invalidation, and load-use hazards sourced from buffered instructions.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
