4s2 (regression, -28.11%): Retiming a tiny BTB into a registered lookahead still adds IF table update/lookup state plus `pred_pc_next` compare plumbing through ID/EX, so avoiding same-cycle PC-select compares did not make CoreMark's taken-branch bubble savings worth the extra fetch/control cost.\nhyp-20261003-003-r5s2\tReturn-address stack predictor\tregression\t107.36\t-6.33\t48.075\t4478102\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_rf\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r5s2 (regression, -6.33%): Even a canonical-return-only RAS predictor adds imem_data compare/RAS-top muxing to IF and predicted-return repair plumbing through ID/EX, so CoreMark's return-bubble savings did not offset the fetch/control-path timing cost.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"One predictor variant still looks underexplored: a single hot backedge predictor driven by a countdown instead of a fetch PC tag compare. That keeps the usual table/tag lookup out of IF; the live PC path would pay mostly a small zero-detect plus target mux, while EX still verifies and repairs the prediction."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r3s2\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, \\\"---\\\", (.changes[] | .file + \\\": \\\" + .description), \\\"---notes---\\\", (.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r3s2\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, \\\"---\\\", (.changes[] | .file + \\\": \\\" + .description), \\\"---notes---\\\", (.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"Add a single-entry loop backedge predictor that remembers the last resolved\ntaken backward conditional branch. IF compares the current PC against the\nstored branch PC tag and, on a hit, advances to the stored target instead of\nPC+4. The target is learned in EX from the normal branch_target calculation,\nso IF does not predecode imem_data or build a target adder. ID/EX carries the\npredicted next PC and a predicted-taken bit; EX compares the resolved next PC\nwith the prediction and redirects only on mismatch, so loop exits and aliases\nrecover through the existing flush path.\n\nThis targets CoreMark's hot loop backedges with much less timing risk than\nthe failed BTB and BTFNT attempts. It should recover most repeated taken-loop\nbubbles while adding only a small registered target, a PC tag compare, and an\nEX-side prediction check. Training only backward conditional branches avoids\npolluting the entry with calls, returns, and forward error paths.\n\n---\nrtl/core_pkg.sv: Add predicted-next-PC and predicted-taken metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles so EX can verify predictions precisely.\nrtl/if_stage.sv: Add the one-entry loop predictor state, PC-tag hit logic, predicted PC advance, and EX-resolve training/clear inputs.\nrtl/id_stage.sv: Propagate prediction metadata from the IF/ID payload into the ID/EX register without changing decode legality behavior.\nrtl/ex_stage.sv: Compute the resolved next PC for every instruction, redirect on prediction mismatch, and emit backward-branch training or loop-exit clear signals.\nrtl/core.sv: Wire prediction metadata and EX training signals between IF, ID, and EX while preserving the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\n---notes---\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
